{"name":"Anarchy","hex":"#de0300","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"anarchy","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The state of a society being without authorities or an authoritative governing body.","The political theory that a community is best organized by the voluntary cooperation of individuals, rather than by a government, which is regarded as being coercive by nature."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/anarchy"}],"entries":[{"title":"Anarchy","blocks":[{"text":"Anarchy is a form of society without rulers. As a type of stateless society, it is commonly contrasted with states, which are polities that claim a monopoly on violence over a permanent territory. Beyond a lack of government, it can more precisely refer to societies that lack any form of authority or hierarchy. While viewed positively by anarchists, the primary advocates of anarchy, it is viewed negatively by advocates of statism, who see it in terms of social disorder."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anarchy","wikidata":"Q31895"},{"title":"Anarchy (international relations)","blocks":[{"text":"In international relations theory, the concept of anarchy is the idea that the world lacks any supreme authority or sovereignty. In an anarchic state, there is no hierarchically superior, coercive power that can resolve disputes, enforce law, or order the system of international politics. In international relations, anarchy is widely accepted as the starting point for international relations theory."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anarchy_(international_relations)","wikidata":"Q2371005"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Immanuel_Kant_%28painted_portrait%29.jpg/500px-Immanuel_Kant_%28painted_portrait%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Immanuel_Kant_%28painted_portrait%29.jpg/500px-Immanuel_Kant_%28painted_portrait%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"German idealist philosopher Immanuel Kant, who looked at anarchy as a thought experiment to justify government","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AImmanuel_Kant_(painted_portrait).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/WilliamGodwin.jpg/500px-WilliamGodwin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/WilliamGodwin.jpg/500px-WilliamGodwin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"English political philosopher William Godwin, an early proponent of anarchy as a political regime","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWilliamGodwin.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Portrait_of_Pierre_Joseph_Proudhon_1865.jpg/500px-Portrait_of_Pierre_Joseph_Proudhon_1865.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Portrait_of_Pierre_Joseph_Proudhon_1865.jpg/1280px-Portrait_of_Pierre_Joseph_Proudhon_1865.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Portrait_of_Pierre_Joseph_Proudhon_1865.jpg/500px-Portrait_of_Pierre_Joseph_Proudhon_1865.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pierre-Joseph Proudhon, the first person to self-identify with the term \"anarchist\" and one of the first to redefine \"anarchy\" in a positive sense","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APortrait_of_Pierre_Joseph_Proudhon_1865.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Bakunin_Nadar.jpg/500px-Bakunin_Nadar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Bakunin_Nadar.jpg/500px-Bakunin_Nadar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mikhail Bakunin, who infused the term \"anarchy\" with simultaneous positive and negative definitions","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABakunin_Nadar.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Robert_Nozick_1977_Libertarian_Review_cover.jpg/500px-Robert_Nozick_1977_Libertarian_Review_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Robert_Nozick_1977_Libertarian_Review_cover.jpg/500px-Robert_Nozick_1977_Libertarian_Review_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Robert Nozick, whose 1974 work Anarchy, State, and Utopia spurred contemporary debates on the viability of anarchy","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARobert_Nozick_1977_Libertarian_Review_cover.jpg"}],"sounds":{"anarchy":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d5/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-anarchy.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/d/d5/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-anarchy.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-anarchy.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/d/d5/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-anarchy.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-anarchy.wav.ogg"},{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/26/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Simplificationalizer-anarchy.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/2/26/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Simplificationalizer-anarchy.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Simplificationalizer-anarchy.wav.mp3"},{"type":